"Four-leaf clover": Your luck has increased. This only happens if you
sacrifice at a time when your prayer timeout is zero (in which case,
you can safely pray if your luck is zero or positive, but may get a
bad result if your luck is negative.)

"Hopeful feeling": You prayer timeout is non-zero - if you pray now,
it will be TOO SOON, and your god will be annoyed at you. He may still
get you out of trouble, but it better be REALLY serious, and if he
does then he'll make you wait an extra long time before you can pray
again. Your god is NOT CURRENTLY angry with you, but it's just too
soon to pray (and he will thus be "displeased" if you do. Sacrificing
reduces your prayer timeout, though, hence the following message...

"Feeling of reconciliation": The value of the sacrifice is sufficient
to reduce your prayer timeout to zero. You can now safely pray if your
luck and your alignment are non-negative.

"Feeling of inadequacy": Your god is angry, and time will NOT cure a
god's anger. You need to sacrifice something bigger to appease him
before you can safely pray. No matter how many smaller sacrifices you
make to him, he won't be appeased - it has to be one (or more) thing
that is over a certain threshold of monster difficulty (which is
higher if you are chaotic and lower if you are lawful.)

"God seems mollified": You sacrificed something big enough to appease
your god's anger when he was angry. You may or may not be able to
safely pray depending on your luck, alignment and prayer timeout.

"God seems partially mollified" - The beast you sacrificed enough was
over the god-appeasing threshold (and thus capable of reducing your
god's anger level), but your god was so angry he requires more than
one mollification sacrifice.

